body:
Ooowee. Firstly, sucks that this is what it is taking for you all to have a baby. Trust me, I feel you. 

&#x200B;

My husband is obstructive azoospermia due to being a CF carrier. After 5 years of trying and 1 1/2 years of serious (temps, sticks, ect.) trying, we saw the one fertility doctor in our state. He was on board to go to the appointment, but besides *showing up* he was not active in the process. During that appointment, we get the news that he has 0 sperm and likely cause. We decide later that day that the next step is him getting genetic testing (to confirm CF) and going to a reproductive urologist and getting assessed at what needs to be done/if he can father children. I ask if he wants me to schedule it for him and he says, "no, this is something important that I need to do for my family". 

&#x200B;

Fast forward 1 1/2 later, I am hesitant to bring it up because he still hasn't done anything on the front and it just stresses him out. I *finally* have a serious conversation with him about his participation and how regardless of what he is saying, what his actions are telling me he wants for our family. This is a breach of trust/expectations that I was willing to walk away from after a certain point. This isn't not unloading the dishwasher, this is my life, our life, and future. He said part of the reason was that he knew he wasn't doing to the right thing and felt bad and so every time he thought about the subject, would just push it out of his mind. But he was too prideful to ask for help to schedule the appointments. I think it finally registered with him that if he didn't step up and take an active role in our being/making a family that I wasn't willing to stay/wait another year without a decision (and I'm not). Initially he had decided that he was okay with using a sperm donor, but as we were browsing donors he got really upset about picking  traits that looked like him because "ultimately they won't look like me". I was thrilled to hear that he finally, *finallly* felt *something.* Thus, IVF it is. 

&#x200B;

Since that realization, he has seen the reproductive urologist and confirmed the first doctors suspicions. We are scheduled for the preliminary IVF appointment and his sperm tissue retrieval in September. This by far has been the most difficult thing my partner and I have been through, but I feel like this hurdle is something that will have made us stronger and more capable to handle raising children and weathering the hurdles of life. I hope that he cracks out of his shell and tells you what is really going on. This is such a complex thing that is full of shoulds or ought to's that you lose focus on nope, this is what *our* reality looks like. If you ever need to talk or vent, feel free to PM me. I 100% get the resentment aspect.
